# A comparison between a list and an int, reached through a pipe that put
# the arguments in a different order from the one the function declares.
#
# Should: be refused before it runs, since `i < 1` needs a number and the
# pipe hands `walk` a list in that position; failing that, the run-time
# error should name the file and line of the `<`.
#
# Does: `kanso play compare_without_location.kso` (with or without
# --interp) stops with
#   error[runtime]: comparison requires two values of one comparable type
# and no file, line, birthplace or trace. In the port the same mistake,
# `options ... . read_options args (i + 1)` in cli/cli.kso, passed
# `kanso check main.kso` and was found by bisecting the program by hand.
fn walk args i acc
  return acc if i < 1 or length args < i
  walk args (i + 1) (acc + 1)

fn start args
  0 . walk args 1

print "{start ["a" "b"]}"
